# Who is guarding what?
teaches: ["tactics/removing-the-defender","tactics/overloading","tactics/deflection","tactics/decoy"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "How do you remove the defender? What does it mean when one piece has too many jobs? How do deflection and decoy pull a piece away?"
next: {"title":"Which mating patterns should you know by sight?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/11-which-mating-patterns-should-you-know-by.cast"}

## Warm-up: a pin and a fork
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r3k2r/pp3p1p/3p2p1/4n3/8/2P5/PPB2PPP/R3R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [f4]}
      - {fen: r5k1/p2q1p1p/1p2b1p1/8/4N3/8/PPQ2PPP/R5K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nf6+, Kg7, Nxd7], highlights: [g8, d7]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: who is guarding what, and how to pull the guard away. First, two warm-ups from the last two days.
    draw position

B: Black's knight on e5 is attacked by the rook, and the pawn guards it. Looks safe.
    highlight piece_e1 piece_e5 piece_d6

A: It can't move: behind it stands the king.
    highlight piece_e8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["A pinned piece can't run.","Attack it with a pawn."] themes ["pin"] voice a teaches ["tactics/pin"]
        right: Pawn to f4 attacks the knight, and it can't move at all: the king is behind it.
        wrong: The knight can't move. What cheap piece can attack it?

A: A pinned piece is frozen. Attack it, and whatever Black does, it falls.
    highlight piece_f4 piece_e5

A: Second one. Black's king is on g8, the queen on d7.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: My knight can check on f6.
    point at.ref sq_f6 gesture circle

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A knight check that hits something big.","Knight to f6."] themes ["fork"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork"]
        right: Knight to f6, check: it hits the king and the queen. The king steps away, and the knight takes the queen: a knight for a queen.
        wrong: Which knight check also attacks the queen?

B: King and queen, both on light squares.
    draw hl_g8 hl_d7 parallel true

A: Both tricks attacked pieces. Today we attack the pieces that do the guarding.
    erase hl_g8 hl_d7

## Take, overload, lure
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1b2rk1/ppq2ppp/4pn2/6B1/8/2PQ4/PP3PPP/RB2R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qxh7+, Nxh7]}
      - {fen: r1b2rk1/ppq2ppp/4pn2/6B1/8/2PQ4/PP3PPP/RB2R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxf6, g6]}
      - {fen: r1b2rk1/ppq2ppp/4pn2/6B1/8/2PQ4/PP3PPP/RB2R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxf6, gxf6, Qxh7#]}
      - {fen: 3r2k1/ppp2ppp/8/3n4/8/8/PP4PP/3RR1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rxd5, Rf8], highlights: [e8]}
      - {fen: 3r2k1/ppp2ppp/8/3n4/8/8/PP4PP/3RR1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rxd5, Rxd5, Re8#]}
      - {fen: 3q1bk1/ppp2pp1/4p3/6N1/5P2/8/PPP3P1/R4K1R w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxf7, Kxf7]}
      - {fen: 3q1bk1/ppp2pp1/4p3/6N1/5P2/8/PPP3P1/R4K1R w - - 0 1, moves: [Rh8+, Kxh8, Nxf7+, Kg8, Nxd8], highlights: [h8]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    counts:
      - d5
      - e8
sources:
    - id: tactics
      title: 'Chess Tactics: 38 Definitions and Examples'
      authors: Chess.com
      year: 2024
      finding: Removing the defender captures, chases or lures away a defending piece; an overloaded piece has two defensive jobs at once; a deflection lures a defender off the square it guards; a decoy lures a piece onto a particular square, usually by a sacrifice.
      url: https://www.chess.com/article/view/chess-tactics--definitions-and-examples

    card title "Take, overload, lure"

A: Now the lesson. White's queen and bishop aim at h7.
    draw position

A: The knight on f6 guards it.
    highlight piece_f6

A: The king can't take on h7: the bishop backs up the queen.
    highlight piece_b1

B: Queen takes h7, check!
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: And the knight takes her. Before you strike, ask what guards the square.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: So take the guard first. Find the move.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Remove the guard." widget chess line true hints ["Which black piece guards h7?","Your bishop on g5."] themes ["removing the defender"] voice a
        right: Bishop takes f6. Black blocks with g6, and loses the knight.
        wrong: Don't attack h7 yet. Attack the piece that guards it.

    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 1 duration 0.4

B: Why doesn't Black take back?
    point at.ref piece_f6 gesture circle

A: If the pawn takes back,
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: the queen takes h7: mate.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

A: That's removing the defender: take the guard, then what it guarded.
    highlight piece_h7

A: Now a guard with two jobs.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Black's knight on d5: attacked once, guarded once. Nothing to win.
    draw count_d5

A: Now count e8: your rook attacks it, and the same rook on d8 guards it.
    draw count_e8 hl_e8 parallel true

A: One piece, two jobs: it's overloaded.
    highlight piece_d8

    erase count_d5 count_e8 hl_e8

    ask "Use the overload." widget chess line true hints ["Take what the rook guards.","Rook takes d5."] themes ["overloading"] voice a
        right: Rook takes d5. Black's rook steps to f8 instead of taking back, so you win the knight.
        wrong: The rook on d8 has two jobs. Make it choose.

B: And if the rook takes back?
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 1 duration 0.4

A: Then it has left the back rank,
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: and rook to e8 is mate. Pulling a guard off its job is called a deflection.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

A: Last idea. Here White's knight eyes f7.
    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Knight takes f7, and it hits the queen!
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: But the king guards f7, and takes it.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

B: Rook to h8, check?
    animate position_shown 6 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: That's just a rook.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: The king has to take it: it's Black's only move.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: Now it stands on h8, off f7, on a fork square.
    draw hl_h8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Finish it." widget chess line 1 hints ["A knight check.","Knight takes f7."] themes ["decoy","fork"] voice a teaches ["tactics/decoy","tactics/fork"]
        right: Knight takes f7, check: it forks the king and the queen.
        wrong: The king stands on h8 now. Which knight check also hits the queen?

A: The king steps away,
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.3

A: and the knight takes the queen: a rook given, a queen and a pawn taken.
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 1.3

A: That's a decoy: a sacrifice pulls a piece onto a square.
    highlight hl_h8

A: A deflection pulls it off one.
    erase hl_h8

## Your turn: find the guard
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Five for five. No guard is safe from you.
    good: Most of them. Keep asking who guards what.
    poor: Guards hide well at first. Look for them before you attack.
    none: The puzzles will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 3r2k1/ppp1pppp/2n5/1B2b3/8/2P3P1/1P3P1P/R3R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxc6, Bxc3]}
      - {fen: r5k1/p1p2ppp/1p2p3/3n1n2/2B5/2P2Q2/PP3PPP/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxd5, exd5, Qxf5]}
      - {fen: r4rk1/pp3ppp/3b4/3p3q/6b1/2P2N2/PP1B1PPP/R2Q1RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Bxf3, h3, Bxd1], flip: true}
      - {fen: r4rk1/p1p2ppp/3b1n2/8/3PP3/8/PPP2PPP/2BR1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [e5]}
      - {fen: r2qrbk1/1ppp1ppp/4p3/p7/2P5/3P4/PP1QPPPP/R3KB1R b - - 0 1, moves: [Bb4, Qxb4, axb4], flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now that you've seen the guard tricks, five puzzles of your own. Find the guard first.
    draw position

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["What guards the bishop on e5?","Your bishop on b5."] themes ["removing the defender"] voice a
        right: Bishop takes c6, the only guard of e5, and Black answers by grabbing c3.
        wrong: Before you attack the bishop, find what guards it.

A: The bishop grabs c3, but the knight is gone. Taking back would have lost the bishop.
    highlight piece_c3

A: Next: count the jobs.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["One black pawn guards two knights.","Bishop takes d5."] themes ["overloading"] voice a
        right: Bishop takes d5, the pawn takes back, and the queen takes the knight on f5: it had no guard left.
        wrong: Which black piece guards two things at once?

B: One pawn couldn't guard two knights.
    highlight piece_f5

A: Now you're Black, and the board turns round. Who guards h2?
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Which white piece guards h2?","Take the knight on f3."] themes ["removing the defender"] voice a
        right: Bishop takes f3. White plays h3, and the bishop takes the queen. Taking back on f3 would have allowed queen takes h2, mate.
        wrong: Your queen and bishop aim at h2. What stops them?

B: The guard is gone, and the queen went with it.
    highlight piece_d1

A: Back to White, and a trick from day eight.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["A pawn can fork.","The pawn on e4."] themes ["fork"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork"]
        right: Pawn to e5 hits the knight and the bishop: one of them falls.
        wrong: Which pawn move attacks two black pieces at once?

A: Last, as Black again: a pin from day nine.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Which white piece stands in front of its king?","Bishop to b4."] themes ["pin"] voice a teaches ["tactics/pin"]
        right: Bishop to b4 pins the queen to the king, so White loses her for a bishop. Here she takes it, and the pawn takes her.
        wrong: Look along the diagonal to White's king.

B: The queen can't step out of the line.
    highlight piece_b4

A: Every piece has a job.
    erase position

A: Find the guard, then take it, overload it or lure it away.
    card title "take · overload · lure (decoy)" style center subtitle "find the guard first"

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower. Then find its first mistake.
    card title "one slow game: 15 + 10" style center subtitle "find mistake 1"

## Where next
end_page: true

    text end_next_kicker "Next" x 500 y 540 font_size 26 style.opacity 0.6
    link end_next href lecture:11 title "Which mating patterns should you know by sight?" form title size 600 x 500 y 420

    link end_again href lecture:10 title "Watch again" form text mark replay open here x 500 y 250 font_size 28
    text end_count "10 of 30" x 500 y 180 font_size 22 style.opacity 0.6
